1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,043
Why does an engine turn fuel into steady motion?

2
00:00:07,043 --> 00:00:13,432
Inside a cylinder, a repeating four-stroke cycle transforms chemical energy into mechanical rotation.

3
00:00:13,432 --> 00:00:17,690
A four-stroke engine centers on a cylinder, piston, connecting rod, crankshaft, intake and exhaust valves, and an ignition or injection system.

4
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:28,218
The piston moves in a straight line, while the connecting rod and crankshaft convert that motion into rotation.

5
00:00:28,218 --> 00:00:35,571
During intake, the intake valve opens and the piston moves downward.

6
00:00:35,571 --> 00:00:38,215
That motion lowers pressure inside the cylinder and draws in air, or an air-fuel mixture in many older engines.

7
00:00:38,215 --> 00:00:43,464
The exhaust valve remains closed.

8
00:00:43,464 --> 00:00:48,463
Both valves close for compression.

9
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:50,830
The piston moves upward and squeezes the trapped charge into a smaller volume.

10
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:55,539
Higher pressure and temperature prepare the mixture for efficient combustion.

11
00:00:55,539 --> 00:00:58,860
At the top of compression, ignition occurs.

12
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,246
A spark plug ignites gasoline mixtures, while diesel engines use heat from compression to ignite injected fuel.

13
00:01:02,246 --> 00:01:09,157
Expanding gases force the piston downward and spin the crankshaft.

14
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:14,072
This is the stroke that produces useful power.

15
00:01:14,072 --> 00:01:21,325
The exhaust valve then opens.

16
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:24,023
As the piston moves upward, it pushes burned gases out of the cylinder.

17
00:01:24,023 --> 00:01:27,264
Once they leave, the cycle can begin again.

18
00:01:27,264 --> 00:01:34,856
A camshaft opens and closes each valve at precise crankshaft angles.

19
00:01:34,856 --> 00:01:39,647
Correct timing keeps the cycle efficient; incorrect timing can cause poor running, knocking, overheating, or backfiring.

20
00:01:39,647 --> 00:01:42,737
Real engines lose energy to heat, friction, pumping losses, and incomplete combustion.

21
00:01:42,737 --> 00:01:49,040
Oil films, cooling passages, piston rings, and clean air protect performance.

22
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:55,204
Worn rings, leaking valves, overheating, and detonation are common failure paths.

23
00:01:55,204 --> 00:02:01,239
The same four-stroke principle powers most cars, motorcycles, generators, and many aircraft.

24
00:02:01,239 --> 00:02:06,525
Understanding the cycle explains why maintenance matters and why engineers seek better efficiency.

25
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:15,230
The idea is simple; the execution depends on precision.
